like physically i don't i don't  actually know next we had movement  punishments uh the main punishment here  being left to right movement right the  what i'm trying to do is is develop a  straight walking gait so any movement  left to right is a direct punishment and  then i did other various punishments  like punishing for wiggles and punishing  for just trying to get the gate to be  more realistic so many people suggested  before doing things like simulating like  lactic acid and stuff like that and i  did find that of all my crazy ideas the  best idea that performed the best was  quite literally just a punishment every  time the servo direction changed so so  the calculation for that is just like 5  is this 5a basically it's every single  movement that was made divided by um the  move punish div and then i just square  this so the move punish div is 80 and  then all movements is actually every  servo direction change from the last 20  steps again this was just yet another  hyper parameter that i tested but 20  steps is one second at 20 hertz so every  single direction change of a servo is a  punishment for the last second  essentially and again i tried all kinds  of different ideas but this seems to be  the one that worked out best so getting  into some of the results now this is a  seven frame stack discrete delta ppo or  ddpo example no movement punishment and  it's a good example of what can happen  if you don't punish jittery movement  along with an example of how these  reinforcement learning algorithms can  find themselves in a sort of local  optimum for rewards  that it's going to be nearly impossible  for that model to ever break away from  ever again into the future  it also looks like it could be a slight  abuse of physics in this case now here  is a more appropriate  gate and walker this is a seven stack  ddppo with jitter punishment and this is  about 10 million steps into training and  just to be clear  the console that you see there the  leftmost number is the total reward and  then the other number is that the reward  for that very specific step  so this isn't the straightest of walkers  but there is a clear gate here it's the  first of the models to really give me  any hope that this this could ever  uh work  and also check out this move about two  million more steps into training that  same model it's beginning to look pretty  good but the upper legs don't really  seem to want to move very much but  honestly models like this are  super rare to get most of the time what  i'm seeing is more like  a true winners like this and he's not  done yet  there we go  as cool as a model that does that uh to  travel might be i'm really after a  regular walking gate  and then there's models like this guy  all i can say here is that reinforcement  learning algorithms have no vanity they  do not care how silly they look  and you might be wondering how this  model ever learned to do this over  something else  and my suspicion is this is probably to  blame  i don't know what's going on there but  again abuse of physics so this is the  model where i finally begin to settle on  most parameters i'm all in on discrete  delta ppo  uh and those parameters i settled on are  the ones that i showed earlier like 20  hertz 80 punish div seven frame stack  uh the deltas that i showed earlier and  so on i like this one a lot it looks  like a realistic gate  uh there's obviously tons of room for  improvement but this shows a lot of  promise and this is about five million  steps  into training  unfortunately this model evolves into  this model about 11 11 million steps in  while the average reward here is better  i'm  not really a big fan it clearly wants to  keep a very low center of gravity  and you can also see it's doing this  sort of like move stop move stop move  stop and it's subtle here  but about 20 million steps in here's our  guy  it's not ideal it's a very reliable and  stable gate but  i'm just not a fan  yeah you can even see that when this  model does trip itself up it is capable  of recovering and continuing on its way  which again is  is cool but i'm just not the biggest fan  of this gate  for the final biddle model for this  video i went ahead and added a  punishment for the z axes  bounce that's been going on and this is  done in the form of a standard deviation  specifically i'm just simply calculating  numpy standard deviation of the z  history uh the reward is then just a  minus equals of the standard deviation  and then i'm dividing again by a  standard  deviation division  similar to the other divisions that i've  done like with movements and stuff  so the standard deviation is applied to  a history of 10.  i actually have not tinkered much with  making that 20 or you know any other  number so 10 is just the one that i went  with i i basically just ran it in the  simulator looked at some of the outputs  and tweaked that until it was a number  that i felt like yeah that's how much i  want to punish it so  probably more r d could be could be done  there  and  honestly this one reminds me of some of  the earlier models before they became  hoppers it's definitely more of a walker  fairly stable good reliable rewards  i think the biggest thing that i notice  now is that body roll  i am tempted to also punish for the role  as well we can access that with the imu  but i'm  i'm just kind of weary about how much  restriction and i do kind of wonder if  we just let these models go for a few  more tens of millions of steps if things  would just smooth out on their own  because it's more efficient  this model is 20 million steps in so far  and the hoppy model  i let continued training and is  currently 65 million steps in still  hopping around so i think to some extent  a natural gait would be naturally found  but it's also true that gates in real  life are indeed determined by limiting  factors like minimizing energy costs and  joint wear and tear and then also just  body stability is definitely a thing it  makes sense so that the animal's head  and brain isn't just bouncing around and  so vision is stable so it's tough for me  to come up with final decisions about a  good balance for for rewards and  restrictions and stuff and every time  you introduce some new punishment you  run the risk of adverse impacts that you  just did not foresee or intend so  anyways definitely still much more trial  and error to  get through i'm definitely still  learning a ton of new information and  new things i also appreciate all the  comments lots of great suggestions and  also just discreet delta ppo is  something that came from a comment i  don't think i would have stumbled on  that on my own so  yeah thanks to everyone for your input  so that's all for now if you'd like to  learn more about neural networks and how  they work there's a book maybe you've  heard of called neural networks from  scratch by myself and daniel kukewa you  can learn more about that at nnfs.io  otherwise i will see you all in another  video  you
 

